<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="0.5" to="0.7" step="0.1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="2" to="4" step="1"/>
</setup>

<p>
  In this exercise, we use Taylor series to estimate the value of the definite integral
  <me>
    \int_0^{$a} \ln(1+x^$k) \, dx
  </me>
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        First, determine the first four nonzero terms of the Taylor series for <m>f(x) = \ln(1+x^$k)</m> and state them in the provided blanks below.
      </p>
        <p sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
          <m>f(x) = </m><answer><shortDescription>first nonzero term in the Taylor series for the natural log of the quantity x to the $k plus 1</shortDescription>x^$k</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>second nonzero term in the Taylor series for the natural log of the quantity x to the $k plus 1</shortDescription>-x^(2*$k)/2</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>third nonzero term in the Taylor series for the natural log of the quantity x to the $k plus 1</shortDescription>x^(3*$k)/3</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>fourth nonzero term in the Taylor series for the natural log of the quantity x to the $k plus 1</shortDescription>-x^(4*$k)/4</answer><m>+ \cdots</m>
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use the Taylor series from (a) and the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to express the value of the definite integral as a series of real numbers.  State the first four nonzero terms of that expression, entering their values exactly.
      </p>
      <p sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
          <m>\int_0^{$a} \ln(1+x^$k) \, dx = </m><answer><shortDescription>first nonzero term in Taylor series for the specified definite integral of natural log of the quantity x to the $k plus 1</shortDescription>$a^($k+1)/($k+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>second nonzero term in Taylor series for the specified definite integral of natural log of the quantity x to the $k plus 1</shortDescription>-$a^(2*$k+1)/(2*(2*$k+1))</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>third nonzero term in Taylor series for the specified definite integral of natural log of the quantity x to the $k plus 1</shortDescription>$a^(3*$k+1)/(3*(3*$k+1))</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>fourth nonzero term in Taylor series for the specified definite integral of natural log of the quantity x to the $k plus 1</shortDescription>-$a^(4*$k+1)/(4*(4*$k+1))</answer><m>+ \cdots</m>
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Observe that the series that represents the value of the definite integral is an alternating series.  If we use the sum of the first four terms of that series to estimate the value of <m>\int_0^{$a} \ln(1+x^$k) \, dx</m>, what is the maximum error of that approximation, according to the Alternating Series Estimation Theorem?
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label>The maximum error is at most </label>$a^(5*$k+1)/(5*(5*$k+1))</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        The value that results from computing the sum of the first four terms of the series in order to approximate the given definite integral is
      </p>
        <p>
          <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>\int_0^{$a} \ln(1+x^$k) \, dx \approx </m></label>$a^($k+1)/($k+1)-$a^(2*$k+1)/(2*(2*$k+1))+$a^(3*$k+1)/(3*(3*$k+1))-$a^(4*$k+1)/(4*(4*$k+1))</answer>
        </p>

    </li>
  </ol>
</p>